hey everyone this is kevin from  thechestwebsite.com and today we're  going to be going over the pin knight  trap many times when you play knight to  f3 your opponent's going to get their  bishop pin down your knight to your  queen  and you don't know what to do so we're  going to go over a trap where you can  actually use that to your advantage this  can happen in lots of different openings  i'm going to show it through the lens of  the king's gamut just because i play a  lot of kings gambit and i see it come up  quite often so the king's game it starts  out with e4 e5  f4  and then we are going to look at the  decline line so bishop c5  knight to f3  and then pawn d6 now this is the  preferred method that bobby fischer used  to play  with this d6 opening up for the light  square bishop a lot of players like to  bring their dark square bishop out of  the pawn chain before they play d6  now we are going to play bishop c4  attacking the pawn on f7  and then they're gonna play bishop g4  getting that light square bishop  involved into the game and pinning our  knight to the queen  one thing i always like to do if my  opponent does pin down my knight to the  queen  is look for a strong attack with my  light square bishop on the f7 square now  you can see right now that it would make  a lot of sense for me to take with my  bishop on f7 they could just take and i  don't really have a strong attack if i  just come to  knight g5 check well then they could  just take  with their queen  yes we can take their queen but then  they take our queen and we're gonna end  up  pretty far behind in  material now if you look at where else  the knight can come to it can come to e5  but then they just take with their pawn  that would be ideal  because as soon as we move our knight  there is a discovered attack with our  queen but the problem is this pawn on d6  it's pretty pesky so knowing that's the  idea  we have the move pawn takes e5  and after they recapture with their pawn  we have now laid the trap and so now we  can actually take with our bishop on f7  and if they were to take with their king  now we can play knight takes e5  check  notice that we are moving our knight out  of the way  we are exposing our queen but only if we  can check our opponent  which if your knight is on f3  as soon as the bishop  sacrifices itself on f7 then when you  come up to either e5 or g5 you are going  to be having a discovered attack with  your queen on the bishop and you are  going to be checking that king now if  they don't want to take our bishop  that's completely fine as well they  could come to f8  if you don't want to take our material  that is a-okay with us  we could always bring our bishop back to  b3 we could bring our rook to f1 we have  multiple options  if they don't take our bishop  now if we come back to this situation  you notice that in the king's gambit yes  we have our pawn on f4 and that allows  us to get this defender from a d6 to  exchange on e5 but if you're not playing  the king's gambit how can this come up  well there's a few ways that it could  one is maybe this queen has moved  there's a lot of situations where black  is going to move this queen from d8 and  as long as it's not protecting the g5  square even if this pawn's not here on  f4 you can come up to g5 after the  exchange so exchange right here the  knight comes to g5 and if the queen's  not defending it you still have the same  concept you don't have to worry about  the pawn on d6 moving away because  knight coming to g5 assuming the queen's  not there is check and you have the  discovered attack with the queen so just  always in your games if they try to pin  down your knight to f3 sometimes people  always play you know h3 i gotta kick the  bishop away  see if you have an opportunity to  sacrifice your bishop on f7 and then  move your knight to either g5 or e5  with a discovered attack with your queen  and you may potentially need to do an  in-between move to get that defender  away so you can freely take a piece on  e5 or move to  g5 but hopefully you guys found this  interesting i see it come up quite a bit  hopefully you'll start to see it in your  own games  thank you to our sponsor skillshare if  you're not familiar this is a website  where you can go and take classes on  pretty much any topic that you would  like i'm currently going through one on  mobile app development i get so many  questions about hey can you take the  content on your website and build a  mobile app so i am trying to learn as  much as possible on making that a  reality  if you are someone that can help you can  feel free to reach out to me and let me  know but definitely learning a lot on  this website and skillshare is also  giving to the first thousand people to  sign up using the link below or using my  code  a free 30-day trial so if you want to  support the channel check out skillshare  with that link or the code the chess  website so again skillshare first  thousand people free month thank you  guys so much for watching this video  hopefully you learned something if you  have other topics you want me to cover  feel free to let me know in the comments  below i'll see you guys next time  you
 

